A||N||4||Jake_Dragon||1985 2.5L to a 1987 2.5L||||1||
Z||000000||Jake_Dragon||06-12-2001||07:31 PM||||I have a 1985 Sports Coupe with a 2.5L I have found a 1987 2.5L and was wondering if I could use my electronics with it. Mine is an automatic if that makes a difference. The seller doesn’t have the computer or wire harness. So I was wondering if I could just use my existing setup. Will the distributor from my current 2.5 fit in the newer motor? What sensors would I need to change? The Price is very low but if I have to buy a computer, harness and a mess of sensors I cant afford it. <P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://web.tampabay.rr.com/jakedragon" TARGET=_blank> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/logo.gif"> </A>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||06-12-2001||07:53 PM||||Not if the '87 is an '87. The '87 & '88's have DIS (Direct Ignition System) and the earlier harness or computer won't work. I have a harness that may be missing a couple of plugs and I MAY have a computer as well.||||reg||1
Z||000002||rockcrawl||06-12-2001||09:23 PM||||Actually if you get the coilpacks and module with the '87, I believe your '85 ECM would run it just fine. You would need to change the plugs for the IAC, TPS, injector, coil, and module. All of the wire colors are the same, but you'd need the plugs for the '87 TBI. I have them if you need them. <P> But, the '87 also uses a different throttle cable, TV cable, heater hose, alternator, AC compressor, belt system, fuel lines, etc., etc.. All of this stuff needs to be dealt with. Certain parts are interchangeable between the two and certain parts aren't. <P> I'm doing ecactly the opposite, putting an '85 engine into an '87, it has become a real PITA and I would never consider doing it again. The car will be a real Frankenstein.<P> Now that I think about it, it would be much easier to upgrade the older car with the newer engine than to do what I'm doing. You can use your '85 intake on the '87 and aviod the throttle cable, fuel line, and heater hose problem. You can use either the '85 or '87 belt and pulley system. going the other way is much more dificult though. Take a look at my post from last week, the response from danny boy sounds like a winner.||||reg||7
Z||000003||theogre||06-13-2001||07:33 PM||||Not happening. Dis motor must have correct ECM. Spark timing is handled much diferantly than distributor motors. You'll need the entire harness and ECM for the motor plus the correct trottle and TV cable for the transmission.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Jake_Dragon||06-13-2001||07:48 PM||||I am going to pass on it, he has other parts if anyone is interested I can forward requests, I don't know this person so I wont post his address.<P>He is located in Tampa Florida.<P>A list of most of the parts I have:<BR>Rear deck lid-silver<BR>Front Hood Scratched-black<BR>Passenger Compartment Top<BR>Rear Bumper Cover-silver, scratched<BR>Front quarter panels<BR>Rear Body including quarter panels<BR>Fender Liners<BR>2 headlight motors for 86 & below<BR>2 rear strut assemblies with discs and control arms<BR>2 Doors complete minus hinges<BR>2 half shafts for TM125 Automatic<BR>Shift quandrant and cable for automatic<BR>87 2.5 litre motor, with intake manifold, throttle body, AC compressor, Water pump,<BR>exhaust manifold, sensors, pulleys are bad and need to be replaced.<BR>42,000 miles on motor.<BR>87 TM125 automatic, 36,000 miles||||reg||1
